Track 2: "Where does the dragonfly keep the map?"
- Story & Context: A young apprentice, Suki, corners the head DJ, Ma-Lay, at the floating tea-stall to ask why their rhythms must always sync with the migration of local wasps. Suki reveals she has been secretly slowing down the tempo to hide her own sorrow, but Ma-Lay reveals that the "hollow frequency" is the only thing keeping the river from swallowing their boats whole. The tension of the confession fills the air with the smell of wet jasmine and burnt resin. "My sorrow is just a gear that refused to turn."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A blurred portrait of a young woman with a traditional head-wrap looking into a mirrored bowl of dark water, light from a floating candle reflecting in her eyes, atmospheric and moody.

Track 3: Carrier Wave Overcrowding
- Story & Context: The legendary myth of the Great Migration describes how the entire floating market once drifted out to sea because the music hit a resonance frequency that liquefied the silt beneath them. Now, every year, the DJs play this final track to remind the river of its limits and the people of their precarious ground. The aftermath of the performance is a stillness so heavy it feels like the air has turned to thick syrup. "The river forgets, but the bells remember the tide."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A surreal wide-angle view of a floating market where the boats are hovering inches above the water, glowing glass bells suspended in mid-air, starlit reflection on the dark surface, epic and mythic.

